Let $T$ be a tetrahedron in “standard” position, with three edges along the three positive coordinate axes in $\text{ℝ}^{3}$, and suppose the vertices are $a\textbf{e}_{1}$, $b\textbf{e}_{2}$, $c\textbf{e}_{3}$, and $0$, where $\begin{bmatrix}
      \textbf{e}_{1} & \textbf{e}_{2} & \textbf{e}_{3}
      \end{bmatrix} = I_{3}$. Find formulas for the barycentric coordinates of an arbitrary point $\textbf{p}$ in $\text{ℝ}^{3}$.﻿

exercise_group.instructions:
In Exercises 29–32, $\textbf{a}$﻿, $\textbf{b}$﻿, and $\textbf{c}$ are noncollinear points in $\text{ℝ}^{2}$ and $\textbf{p}$ is any other point in $\text{ℝ}^{2}$﻿. Let $\Delta\textbf{abc}$ denote the closed triangular region determined by $\textbf{a}$, $\textbf{b}$, and $\textbf{c}$﻿, and let $\Delta\textbf{pbc}$ be the region determined by $\textbf{p}$,﻿ $\textbf{b}$﻿, and $\textbf{c}$﻿. For convenience, assume that $\textbf{a}$,﻿ $\textbf{b}$﻿, and $\textbf{c}$ are arranged so that $\det\begin{bmatrix}
      \widetilde{\mathbf{a}} & \widetilde{\mathbf{b}} & \widetilde{\mathbf{c}}
      \end{bmatrix}$ is positive, where $\overset{\sim}{\textbf{a}}$, $\overset{\sim}{\textbf{b}}$﻿, and $\widetilde{\textbf{c}}$ are the standard homogeneous forms for the points.

statement.paragraph:
Show that the area of $\Delta\textbf{abc}$ is $\det{\text{[}\begin{matrix}
      \overset{\sim}{\mathbf{a}} & \overset{\sim}{\mathbf{b}} & \overset{\sim}{\mathbf{c}}
      \end{matrix}\text{]}}/2$. [Hint: Consult Sections 3.2 and 3.3, including the Exercises.]

Let $\textbf{p}$ be a point on the line through $\textbf{a}$ and $\textbf{b}$. Show that $\det{\text{[}\begin{matrix}
      \overset{\sim}{\mathbf{a}} & \overset{\sim}{\mathbf{b}} & \overset{\sim}{\mathbf{p}}
      \end{matrix}\text{]}} = 0$.﻿

Let $\textbf{p}$ be any point in the interior of $\Delta\textbf{abc}$, with barycentric coordinates $(r,s,t)$, so that$\begin{matrix}
      {\text{[}\begin{matrix}
      \overset{\sim}{\mathbf{a}} & \overset{\sim}{\mathbf{b}} & \overset{\sim}{\mathbf{c}}
      \end{matrix}\text{]}} & \begin{bmatrix}
      r \\
      s \\
      t
      \end{bmatrix} & = & \overset{\sim}{\mathbf{p}}
      \end{matrix}$

Take $\textbf{q}$ on the line segment from $\textbf{b}$ to $\textbf{c}$ and consider the line through $\textbf{q}$ and $\textbf{a}$, which may be written as $\textbf{p} = (1 - x)\textbf{q} + x\textbf{a}$ for all real $x$. Show that, for each $x$, $\det\text{[}\begin{matrix}
      \overset{\sim}{\mathbf{p}} & \overset{\sim}{\mathbf{b}} & \overset{\sim}{\mathbf{c}}
      \end{matrix}\text{]} = x \cdot \det\text{[}\begin{matrix}
      \overset{\sim}{\mathbf{a}} & \overset{\sim}{\mathbf{b}} & \overset{\sim}{\mathbf{c}}
      \end{matrix}\text{]}$. From this and earlier work, conclude that the parameter $x$ is the first barycentric coordinate of $\textbf{p}$. However, by construction, the parameter $x$ also determines the relative distance between $\textbf{p}$ and $\textbf{q}$ along the segment from $\textbf{q}$ to $\textbf{a}$. (When $x = 1\text{,}$ $\textbf{p} = \textbf{a}$.) When this fact is applied to Example 5, it shows that the colors at vertex $\textbf{a}$ and the point $\textbf{q}$ are smoothly interpolated as $\textbf{p}$ moves along the line between $\textbf{a}$ and $\textbf{q}$.﻿

Let $\textbf{v}_{1} = ~\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      3 \\
      {- 6}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,$, $\textbf{v}_{2} = ~\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      7 \\
      3 \\
      {- 5}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,$, $\textbf{v}_{3} = ~\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      3 \\
      9 \\
      {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,$, $\textbf{a} = ~\begin{bmatrix}
      0 \\
      0 \\
      9
      \end{bmatrix}\,$, $\textbf{b} = ~\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1.4 \\
      1.5 \\
      {- 3.1}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,$, and $\textbf{x}(t) = \textbf{a} + t\textbf{b}$ for $t \geq 0$. Find the point where the ray $\textbf{x}(t)$ intersects the plane that contains the triangle with vertices $\textbf{v}_{1}$, $\textbf{v}_{2}$, and $\textbf{v}_{3}$. Is this point inside the triangle?

Repeat Exercise 33 with $\textbf{v}_{1} = ~\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      2 \\
      {- 4}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,$, $\textbf{v}_{2} = ~\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
